          "answer": "The triage tool returned \"emergency\" for your situation. That match was based on the words \"chest pain\" in my screening notes, not in your message, so it may not apply to you. If you have chest discomfort, call emergency services now and do not drive yourself. **If you have any of the following right now, call emergency services immediately:**\n\n- Chest pain, pressure or tightness, or breathlessness, sweating or nausea with discomfort. Heart attack symptoms are not always chest pain.\n- A sudden severe headache with no known cause.\n- Sudden face drooping, arm or leg weakness or numbness, slurred speech, sudden confusion, sudden vision loss or blurring, or sudden trouble walking.\n\nIf none of these are present, the guidance I retrieved does not describe a same-day emergency for a home reading of 155/95 alone. However, a reading that stays this high after stopping treatment should be discussed with your clinician soon. Contact the clinician who prescribed your tablets, ideally today or within a day or two, and ask whether you should restart and whether your readings need rechecking. Do not restart or change any medicine until a clinician has advised you. Keep a log of your readings with dates and times to share with them.\n\nI could not find guidance that sets a reading threshold for emergencies, and I found no passages on treatment or follow-up after stopping medication, so I won't fill that gap from memory.\n\nThe cardiology guidance I found (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line* and *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*) recommends reassessment about monthly after treatment starts or changes, until the target is reached. Once readings are stable, it suggests reviews every three to six months. These passages cover starting or changing treatment, not stopping it, so they don't tell you how often you should be checked now. The same goes for when to restart: the passages don't say, 